{
  "name": "What to Do When Your Cabin's Finish Is Failing",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "If you're noticing your log or cabin home's finish isn't holding up like it used to, it’s definitely time to take action. Ignoring a failing finish can lead to bigger problems down the line, so addressing it promptly is key. Here's a quick guide on what steps to consider:",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2",
      "text": "1. **Inspect Your Logs Carefully:** Take a good look at all surfaces, especially those exposed to the most sun and weather. Are there areas where the finish is peeling, flaking, or cracking? Is the wood itself looking gray or rough?",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3",
      "text": "2. **Check for Water Absorption:** Splash a little water on the logs. If it soaks in rather than beading up, your finish is no longer protecting the wood as it should.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4",
      "text": "3. **Assess the Damage:** If the failing finish is widespread, you might need a more thorough approach than just a touch-up. Minor issues might be fixable with sanding, but extensive failure often means full removal.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5",
      "text": "4. **Consider Professional Help:** If you have extensive peeling, signs of moisture intrusion, or previous coatings that have really failed, this isn't a DIY job. Surfaces that need blasting or significant repair before refinishing definitely call for an expert.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 6",
      "text": "Getting professional advice can help you determine the best course of action and ensure the job is done right the first time. We're here to help you restore your cabin's beauty and protection.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 6
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
